{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And it bred worms, etc.</b> The reason it placed ״וַיָּרֻם״ etc. before ״וַיִּבְאַשׁ״ is because the raising of worms is found in a sweet substance and it does not become putrid, since putrefaction is not customary in sweet things in the manner that the generation of worms is customary — and on this account its nature is like a צַפִּיחִת בִּדְבַשׁ (a swelling abscess in honey). Therefore it said <b>and it bred worms</b>, and not only this but also <b>and it became putrid</b>." "And the faithful witness to this explanation of ours is His saying (Exodus 16:24) ״וְלֹא הִבְאִישׁ וְרִמָּה לֹא הָיְתָה בּוֹ״ — meaning, not only did it not become putrid, but even a worm, which normally could exist without putrefaction, was not in it. And if worms would not breed until it had already become putrid, once the verse said ״וְלֹא הִבְאִישׁ״ there would be no need to say further ״וְרִמָּה״ etc." "Now, I have seen that our Sages of blessed memory (Mekhilta) did not explain as we did; perhaps it is because they maintain that in any event the growth of the worm would precede by some slight measure the putrefaction that would ensue from it, even though the sweet substance would not become putrid." "And by way of remez (homiletical allusion), it further intends that a worm breeds only from what has become putrid. Now, we have found that Onkelos translated רַע — בִּישׁ, and the dimension of evil and its root is through the gate of Torah and mitzvah, and when a person sins, worms breed — and this is the sod (mystery) of the breeding of worms from fruits and the air of the earth after a person's sin. And this is the meaning of its saying <b>and it bred worms</b> — the reason is on account of ״וַיִּבְאַשׁ״, that the dimension of evil was made in it because they left it contrary to the mitzvah of the Torah. And therefore when Moses said to them, leave it etc. until the morning, the verse says ״וַיַּנִּיחוּ אוֹתוֹ עַד הַבֹּקֶר כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה מֹשֶׁה וְלֹא הִבְאִישׁ וְרִמָּה לֹא״ etc. — meaning, the dimension of evil did not touch it, and in this respect ״וְרִמָּה לֹא הָיְתָה״; and likewise the righteous, who do not let their soul taste of the dimension of evil, will not breed worms. Go forth and learn from the deed of Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai, upon him be peace (Bava Metzi'a 84b), that a single worm alone grew in his flesh on account of the dimension of evil that had touched him there."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:59.435749387Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}